# What `railway up` uploads, which is not the same question as what the image needs.
#
# `railway.json` builds from the `Dockerfile`, and that Dockerfile copies exactly four things:
# `requirements.txt`, `backend/`, `frontend/` and `docker-entrypoint.sh`. Everything else in this
# checkout is a test environment, local state, or the mobile client - and none of it belongs in an
# upload. The 88 MB model under `backend/models/` is the one large artefact that has to travel;
# `mobile-client/node_modules` alone is 120 MB of files no layer will ever read, and hashing it
# turns a two-minute deploy into a fifteen-minute one.
#
# It mirrors `.dockerignore` deliberately, and for the same reason that file gives: a file that
# travels to the builder but is never copied into the image is a file whose contents were handed
# over for nothing. Here that includes local data (`times.db`) and the one genuine secret in the
# checkout (`deploy/railway/variables.env`, which holds the VAPID private key).

# The mobile client is a separate artefact, built and shipped on its own.
mobile-client/

# The test stack: the suite and its browser never run on the builder.
backend/tests/
backend/requirements-dev.txt

# Local state and biometric data - never uploaded.
times.db
times.db-wal
times.db-shm
*.db.bak
backend/certs/
worker_photos/
local_references/
punch_frames/
quick_link_photos/
calibration_corpus/
coverage_reports/
temp/
backups/

# Development machinery and scratch files from the checkout.
.freebuff/
.vscode/
.git/
docs/
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
*.bak
